  "markup": "\u003Ch2\u003EHeart failure and cardiomyopathy\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EPage highlights\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPeople living with heart failure:\u003C/strong\u003E An estimated 146,000 Australians aged 18 and over (0.7%) were living with heart failure in 2022\u201324.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHospitalisations:\u003C/strong\u003E Around 188,000 hospitalisations in 2023\u201324 had heart failure or cardiomyopathy recorded as a principal and/or additional diagnosis.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDeaths:\u003C/strong\u003E In 2024, heart failure or cardiomyopathy was the underlying cause of 5,200 deaths and an associated cause of 22,400 deaths.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EWhat are heart failure and cardiomyopathy?\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cp\u003EHeart failure occurs when the heart becomes less effective at pumping blood around the body. It can result from conditions that damage or overload the heart, including heart attack, high blood pressure, damaged heart valves and cardiomyopathy.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECardiomyopathy is a weakening of all or a large part of the heart muscle. Causes include coronary heart disease, high blood pressure, viral infections and drinking alcohol above guideline levels. The two conditions commonly occur together.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EMild heart failure may cause few symptoms. More severe cases can cause ongoing tiredness, shortness of breath and reduced ability to be physically active. Heart failure usually can\u0027t be cured because the heart damage is permanent, but treatment can improve quality of life, reduce hospitalisations and extend life.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EHow many Australians are living with heart failure?\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cp\u003EBased on self-reported data from the 2022\u201324 National Health Measures Survey:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAn estimated 146,000 adults (0.7%) were living with heart failure.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAlmost two-thirds (63%, or 91,600 people) were aged 65 and over.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAn estimated 84,800 men (0.9%) and 61,500 women (0.6%) were living with the condition.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThese figures likely underestimate the true number. Early heart failure may cause only mild symptoms, and many cases are undiagnosed.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EHospitalisations\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cp\u003EHeart failure and cardiomyopathy often occur alongside other chronic diseases, so hospitalisation estimates include both principal and additional diagnoses. The true numbers are likely higher because heart failure has historically been under-recorded in hospital data, and coding accuracy varies between hospitals.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIn 2023\u201324, there were around 188,000 hospitalisations involving heart failure or cardiomyopathy. This equals 698 per 100,000 population and 1.5% of all hospitalisations in Australia. Heart failure or cardiomyopathy was the principal diagnosis in 38% (71,200) of these hospitalisations.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EWhen it was an additional diagnosis, the principal diagnosis was cardiovascular disease in 30% of cases and respiratory disease in 23%. The most common principal diagnoses were:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EInfluenza or pneumonia (9.1%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECoronary heart disease (8.7%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EChronic lower respiratory diseases (7.1%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAtrial fibrillation or flutter (6.1%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch4\u003EAge and sex\u003C/h4\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAfter adjusting for age, hospitalisation rates were 1.4 times as high for males as for females. Rates increased with age and were highest among people aged 85 and over.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch4\u003ETrends\u003C/h4\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe age-standardised hospitalisation rate fell by 31% between 2000\u201301 and 2023\u201324, with a similar decline among males and females.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch4\u003EDifferences between population groups\u003C/h4\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIn 2023\u201324, age-standardised hospitalisation rates were:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy socioeconomic area:\u003C/strong\u003E 1.8 times as high in the lowest socioeconomic areas as in the highest (1.7 times for males and 1.9 times for females).\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy remoteness:\u003C/strong\u003E 1.7 times as high in Remote and very remote areas as in Major cities (1.4 times for males and 2.1 times for females).\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Cp\u003EUnlike in other remoteness areas, rates in Remote and very remote areas were higher among females than males.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EDeaths\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cp\u003EHeart failure and cardiomyopathy contributed to around 27,600 deaths in 2024, or 15% of all deaths. This equals 102 per 100,000 population. They were the underlying cause of around 5,200 deaths and an associated cause of around 22,400.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThey are more often listed as an associated cause because a complication or related condition is often recorded as the underlying cause on the death certificate. In these cases, the most common underlying causes were:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECoronary heart disease (17%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EChronic obstructive pulmonary disease (8.7%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDiabetes (5.7%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDementia, including Alzheimer\u0027s disease (5.3%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECardiac arrhythmias (5.0%)\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch4\u003EAge and sex\u003C/h4\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAfter adjusting for age, death rates in 2024 were 1.4 times as high for males as for females, and age-specific rates were higher for males in every age group. Rates also increased with age, and 84% of deaths were among people aged 75 and over.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003ERates were highest among people aged 85 and over. Compared with people aged 75\u201384 of the same sex, they were 5.1 times as high for males and 7.4 times as high for females.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch4\u003ETrends\u003C/h4\u003E\u003Cp\u003EBetween 1997 and 2024:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDeaths with heart failure or cardiomyopathy as an underlying or associated cause rose by 33%, from 20,800 to 27,600.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAge-standardised death rates fell by 45% among males and 43% among females.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDeath rates rose in 2022, reflecting higher overall mortality during the COVID-19 pandemic, and have since returned to pre-pandemic levels.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch4\u003EDifferences between population groups\u003C/h4\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIn 2024, age-standardised death rates were:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy socioeconomic area:\u003C/strong\u003E 1.6 times as high in the lowest socioeconomic areas as in the highest, with a similar gap for males and females.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy remoteness:\u003C/strong\u003E 1.6 times as high in Remote and very remote areas as in Major cities. Rates were higher for males than females in all remoteness areas.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch3\u003EMore information\u003C/h3\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ca data-router-slot=\u0022disabled\u0022 href=\u0022https://www.aihw.gov.au/reports/heart-stroke-vascular-diseases/hsvd-facts/contents/all-heart-stroke-and-vascular-disease/heart-failure-and-cardiomyopathy\u0022 type=\u0022external\u0022\u003Efull AIHW report\u003C/a\u003E includes information on First Nations people and supplementary data tables.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cem\u003ESource: Australian Institute of Health and Welfare (AIHW), Heart failure and cardiomyopathy. Prevalence data is based on AIHW analysis of ABS 2025.\u003C/em\u003E\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </bodyContent>
    <category>
